# Commands this deployment's remediation executor may run once a person has
# approved them: one full-match regex per line; `#` starts a comment. The file
# is hot-read — checked over the WHOLE procedure at the click and again before
# every step, so a list tightened mid-incident stops the steps that have not
# run yet. Deny by default: absent, empty or unreadable all mean proposals
# collect and nothing executes.
#
# Copy to remediation-allowlist beside this file (git-ignored: the real lines
# name real commands) and set, in the deployment's .env,
#   HOOKPROBE_REMEDIATION_ALLOWLIST=/etc/hookprobe/operator/remediation-allowlist
# — the work compose mounts this directory there, read-only, on the one node
# that executes: the write node, the service running `HOOKPROBE_BASH_GUARD:
# danger-only` with the scoped credential.
#
# A step the report itself called `risk: high` must full-match a pattern in a
# SECOND file as well — remediation-high-risk-allowlist beside this one, named
# by HOOKPROBE_REMEDIATION_HIGH_RISK_ALLOWLIST — so arming the executor does
# not arm its worst half by the same gesture. The risk label is the model's,
# so that file can only add a requirement, never catch a missing one.
#
# Full-match, and each command runs as an argv with no shell: a wildcard
# cannot hand anything to /bin/sh, but `.*` is still a line you wrote wide.
# Write the command you mean, and start narrower than you think: the quickstart
# arms its rehearsal with two observation lines, and that is the shape to keep
# — the fewest lines that do the job.
^df -h /data$
^du -sh /data/results$
